# json — JSON, in pure Luce.
#
# Parse text into a value, ask a value what it is with `match`, build
# values directly, write one back out.  Nothing here is a builtin,
# nothing here imports anything, and nothing here touches the host:
# `parse` takes a string and answers a `Json`, and where the string
# came from is the caller's business.
#
# **A JSON value is a union, because that is what a JSON value is.**
# RFC 8259 says a value is one of six things and two of the six
# contain more values; `union Json` says the same sentence in Luce, and
# `match` is the only way to read it — so asking a number for its
# members is not a checked error, it is a program that does not
# compile.  This module is the customer `docs/UNION.md` was argued
# from, and it is now written on top of it.
#
#     match doc:
#         object(fields):
#             let port = fields.get("port")
#             ...
#
# **Eager, and the value is the answer.**  A parse turns `"1e3"` into
# 1000.0 and `"é"` into é once, on the way in, and what a caller
# holds afterwards is the number and the text rather than a span
# pointing back at the document.  The document's bytes are not kept.
# That is the trade this module makes and it is worth naming: the
# previous design was lazy — every value remembered the text it was
# written as, and `write` put those exact tokens back — because
# without unions a value had to be an index into a flat tape.  With a
# union the value *is* the value, so `write` re-encodes rather than
# echoes, and the property it keeps is the one that matters:
# **parsing what `write` produced gives an equal value, and writing
# that gives identical text.**
#
# **Ownership is scope ownership, with nothing new in it.**  A `Json`
# carries objects — `list(Json)` and `map(string, Json)` are heap
# objects — so it takes `give`/`copy` where any carrying value does
# (OWNERSHIP.md S24, S27) and it dies with the binding that received
# it.  Freeing a document is freeing one value; the containers inside
# it go with it, recursively (S20).  There is no arena, no collector
# and no `deinit` to remember.
#
# **Building one reads the way taking one apart does.**  The map and
# the list *are* the builder, so ownership is taken once, at the
# outermost value:
#
#     var fields = new map(string, Json)
#     fields["name"] = Json.text(value = "luce")
#     fields["tags"] = Json.array(items = [Json.integer(value = 1)])
#     let doc = Json.object(fields = give fields)
#
# Every inner value there is fresh and silent (S20); the one `give` is
# on the last line, because that is the one place a named object moves
# into a value that outlives the name.
#
# **Written against the normative documents.**  The grammar is RFC
# 8259 — §2 the structural characters and the four whitespace bytes,
# §3 the three literal names, §4 objects, §5 arrays, §6 numbers, §7
# strings and their escapes, §8.1 encoding, §8.2 unpaired surrogates,
# §9 nesting limits.  ECMA-404 says the same grammar in other words
# and is cited where the two documents leave a reader a choice.  Every
# rule below names the clause it implements, and the specification
# (`src/luce/specs/json_spec.zig`) proves each one on both engines.
#
# **Reading a file** is three calls, and the module deliberately makes
# none of them:
#
#     let bytes = try files.read_bytes(path)
#     let text = strings.from_bytes(bytes) else ""
#     let doc = try json.parse(text)
#
# **UTF-8 needs no checking here** (RFC 8259 §8.1).  The input is a
# Luce string, which is valid UTF-8 by construction, so the encoding
# question every other parser has to answer was answered before the
# text arrived.  What is left is the grammar, and that is all this
# module checks.
#
# **The calls this module makes that other parsers argue about**, each
# with its reason:
#
# * **A number is read by the notation it was written in.**  JSON has
#   one number type and Luce has two, and a language with no implicit
#   narrowing cannot hand a `long` out of a `double` without inventing
#   or discarding information.  So `42` is `Json.integer` and `4.2`,
#   `42.0` and `4.2e1` are all `Json.real` — the rule `as_long` used
#   to enforce by re-reading the text is now the member the value
#   holds, checked by the compiler.  Zig's `std.json`, serde_json,
#   Jackson and System.Text.Json all split the same way and for the
#   same reason.  A whole number too large for a `long` is a `real`,
#   which is where its precision honestly is.
# * **A number past what a `double` can hold is refused**, by name, at
#   the byte (RFC 8259 §6: *"This specification allows implementations
#   to set limits on the range and precision of numbers accepted"*).
#   The alternative, now that values are eager, would be storing an
#   infinity — and infinity is not JSON, so it could never be written
#   back.  `1e309` is an error with a sentence; `1e308` is a number.
# * **Nesting is bounded at 64** (RFC 8259 §9 explicitly allows a
#   parser to set a limit).  A tree is walked by recursion — this
#   module's own parser and writer recurse once per level, and so will
#   every caller that reads one — and loom lets a Luce program nest
#   128 calls before it traps `call_depth_exceeded`
#   (`src/apps/host.zig`).  Sixty-four is half of that: a document
#   this module accepts can be parsed, walked and written from a call
#   stack that is already sixty deep.  The old bound was 128, chosen
#   when the parser was iterative and only the *caller* recursed;
#   a union has no iterative walk to offer, so the number moved to
#   stay true.
# * **A duplicate member name resolves to the last one written**
#   (RFC 8259 §4: names SHOULD be unique, and the behaviour when they
#   are not is unpredictable).  An object is a `map(string, Json)`, so
#   the second `"a"` replaces the first in place — the same thing
#   JavaScript's `JSON.parse`, Python's `json` and Go's
#   `encoding/json` all do, and unlike the previous design the
#   document does *not* keep both: a mapping with two entries under
#   one key is not a mapping.  Order is insertion order, so a member's
#   position is where it was first named.
# * **An unpaired surrogate escape is refused** (RFC 8259 §8.2 warns
#   about them; ECMA-404 permits the code unit sequence).  A Luce
#   string is UTF-8 and there is no UTF-8 for half a surrogate pair,
#   so the honest answers are "refuse" or "quietly substitute U+FFFD",
#   and this module refuses by name at the byte where it happened.
#   A well-formed pair — `𝄞` — is one codepoint, 𝄞, and is
#   read as one.
# * **NaN and Infinity are not JSON** (RFC 8259 §6 lists exactly one
#   number grammar and no names in it), so they are refused like any
#   other unknown word on the way in — and a `Json.real` built by hand
#   out of one traps on the way out, because there is no text this
#   module could write that would read back as what it was given.
#
# Errors name a byte offset, because that is the position the parser
# genuinely has and a line and column would be a second thing to keep
# right.  `parse` answers `Json!` and not `Json?` because there are
# many distinct ways for a document to be wrong and the one it met is
# worth carrying (docs/FAILURE.md).

# --- the vocabulary of the grammar -----------------------------------
#
# RFC 8259 §2: the six structural characters, the two quoting bytes,
# and nothing else in this module is written as a bare number.

private const quote_byte: byte = 34
private const backslash: byte = 92
private const brace_open: byte = 123
private const brace_close: byte = 125
private const bracket_open: byte = 91
private const bracket_close: byte = 93
private const colon: byte = 58
private const comma: byte = 44
private const minus: byte = 45
private const plus: byte = 43
private const point: byte = 46
private const digit_zero: byte = 48
private const digit_nine: byte = 57
private const letter_e: byte = 101
private const letter_e_capital: byte = 69
private const letter_u: byte = 117
private const letter_i: byte = 105
private const letter_n: byte = 110

# RFC 8259 §9 leaves the limit to the parser.  This one is half of
# loom's call-depth policy, because a tree is walked by recursion and
# both ends of that walk — this module and its caller — have to fit
# inside the one budget.
private const max_depth = 64

# --- what a value is -------------------------------------------------

# One JSON value (RFC 8259 §3 the literal names, §4 objects, §5
# arrays, §6 numbers, §7 strings).
#
# Seven members for six kinds: JSON's one number type arrives here as
# the two Luce has, and which one a value holds is the notation it was
# written in.  `null` is first, so it is also the zero — what a
# `var j: Json` starts at and what fills a `new array(Json, n)`.
#
# The names are the specification's own except for the string kind:
# `string` is a reserved name in Luce (it is the type, and the
# conversion constructor named for it), so JSON's string is spelled
# `text` here — the same word `strings.from_bytes` uses for the same
# thing.
union Json:
    null
    boolean(value: bool)
    integer(value: long)
    real(value: double)
    text(value: string)
    array(items: list(Json))
    object(fields: map(string, Json))

    # True for JSON's `null` and nothing else — which is a different
    # question from "is this value missing", and that is the point of
    # having both (`member` answers absence).
    func is_null() -> bool:
        match self:
            null:
                return true
            else:
                return false

    # `true` or `false`; absent for every other member.
    func as_bool() -> bool?:
        match self:
            boolean(value):
                return value
            else:
                return none

    # The number as a whole number — absent unless it was written as
    # one.  `42` answers 42; `4.2`, `42.0` and `4.2e1` all answer
    # `none` and are read with `as_double`.  A number written as a
    # real is a real, and answering absence says so, where truncating
    # would throw information away silently and rounding would invent
    # it.
    func as_long() -> long?:
        match self:
            integer(value):
                return value
            else:
                return none

    # The number as a double, whichever way it was written — a whole
    # number widens, which is the one conversion Luce makes on its own
    # (docs/TYPES.md).
    func as_double() -> double?:
        match self:
            integer(value):
                return double(value)
            real(value):
                return value
            else:
                return none

    # The string, decoded — the escapes were spent on the way in, so
    # this is the text the document meant.  Absent for every other
    # member.
    func as_text() -> string?:
        match self:
            text(value):
                return value
            else:
                return none

    # How many members an object has, or elements an array has.  A
    # leaf answers 0.
    func count() -> long:
        match self:
            array(items):
                return len(items)
            object(fields):
                return len(fields)
            else:
                return 0

    # The member of an object named `name`, or absent — for a name
    # that is not there and for a value that is not an object, which
    # are the same news to a caller who was looking for a field.
    #
    # **The answer is a copy**, and deliberately so: a value read out
    # of a container has an owner already (OWNERSHIP.md S17, S22), and
    # the only thing a function can hand back is one of its own.  For
    # a leaf that is a number or a string; for a subtree it is the
    # whole subtree.  A walk that only means to *read* should say
    # `match` and reach the map itself, which copies nothing.
    func member(name: string) -> Json?:
        match self:
            object(fields):
                if fields.has(name):
                    return fields[name]
                return none
            else:
                return none

    # The element at `index` of an array, or the member at that
    # position of an object.  Out of range traps: `count()` is right
    # there, and asking past it is a bug rather than news
    # (docs/FAILURE.md) — which is the same split the language's own
    # containers make, where `xs[i]` traps and `m.get(k)` does not.
    #
    # A copy, for `member`'s reason — and on an object it also walks
    # the names to find the position, so a loop that wants every
    # member should `match` and iterate the map once instead.
    func element(index: long) -> Json:
        match self:
            array(items):
                if index < 0 or index >= len(items):
                    trap(f"json: no element {index} in a value of {len(items)}")
                return items[index]
            object(fields):
                let names = fields.keys()
                if index < 0 or index >= len(names):
                    trap(f"json: no element {index} in a value of {len(names)}")
                return fields[names[index]]
            else:
                trap(f"json: no element {index} in a value of 0")

    # The value as JSON text, with nothing between the tokens.
    #
    # Not an echo of whatever text this value was parsed from — the
    # value is what survived that parse — but a re-encoding of the
    # value itself, so `parse(j.write())` is a value equal to `j` and
    # writing *that* gives the same bytes again.
    func write() -> string:
        var out = new builder()
        put_value(out, self, 0, 0)
        return out.build()

    # The same, indented: every member and element on its own line,
    # `spaces` further in than its container.  `spaces` of 0 is
    # `write`.
    func pretty(spaces: long) -> string:
        if spaces < 0:
            trap("json: pretty needs spaces >= 0")
        var out = new builder()
        put_value(out, self, spaces, 0)
        return out.build()

# --- parsing ---------------------------------------------------------

# Read one JSON document (RFC 8259 §2: exactly one value, with
# whitespace allowed around it).
#
# Everything that can be wrong with the text is an error naming the
# byte it happened at.
func parse(content: string) -> Json!:
    let start = skip_blank(content, 0)
    if start >= len(content):
        error("json: there is no value in the text")
    let value, next = try read_value(content, start, 0)
    let after = skip_blank(content, next)
    if after != len(content):
        error(f"json: text after the document's one value, at byte {after}")
    return value

# One value, and the byte after it.
#
# Recursive descent: the shape of the grammar is the shape of the
# walk, and a union has no flat tape to walk instead.
#
# **The two container grammars are inlined here rather than written as
# `read_array` and `read_object`, and the reason is `max_depth`.**  A
# nested value would otherwise cost *two* frames a level — the reader
# for the container and the reader for the value inside it — and the
# budget this module has to divide is loom's 128 nested calls, shared
# with whatever the caller does with the answer.  One frame a level is
# what lets the bound be a number worth having.  This is the one place
# in the module where a function is longer than its parts because the
# parts would cost something real (docs/CODING_GUIDE.md).
private func read_value(content: string, from: long, depth: long) -> (Json, long)!:
    let at = skip_blank(content, from)
    if at >= len(content):
        error(f"json: a value was expected at byte {at}")
    let head = content.byte_at(at)

    # RFC 8259 §5: values separated by commas, between brackets.
    if head == bracket_open:
        if depth >= max_depth:
            error(f"json: values nested deeper than {max_depth}, at byte {at}")
        var items = new list(Json)
        var in_list = skip_blank(content, at + 1)
        # The loop leaves with `in_list` on the closing bracket,
        # whichever way it got there, so the value is built once at the
        # bottom — which is also what keeps `give items` to the one
        # place S29 wants it.
        if in_list >= len(content) or content.byte_at(in_list) != bracket_close:
            var listed = false
            while not listed:
                let element, after = try read_value(content, in_list, depth + 1)
                items.append(element)
                in_list = skip_blank(content, after)
                if in_list >= len(content):
                    error(f"json: the text ends inside the array opened at byte {at}")
                let mark = content.byte_at(in_list)
                if mark == comma:
                    in_list += 1
                elif mark == bracket_close:
                    listed = true
                else:
                    error(f"json: a comma or a close was expected at byte {in_list}")
        return Json.array(items = items), in_list + 1

    # RFC 8259 §4: names, colons, values, commas, between braces.
    if head == brace_open:
        if depth >= max_depth:
            error(f"json: values nested deeper than {max_depth}, at byte {at}")
        var fields = new map(string, Json)
        var in_map = skip_blank(content, at + 1)
        if in_map >= len(content) or content.byte_at(in_map) != brace_close:
            var mapped = false
            while not mapped:
                in_map = skip_blank(content, in_map)
                if in_map >= len(content) or content.byte_at(in_map) != quote_byte:
                    error(f"json: a member name must be a string, at byte {in_map}")
                let name, after_name = try read_string(content, in_map)
                in_map = skip_blank(content, after_name)
                if in_map >= len(content) or content.byte_at(in_map) != colon:
                    error(f"json: a member name must be followed by a colon, at byte {in_map}")
                let member, after = try read_value(content, in_map + 1, depth + 1)
                # §4 leaves a repeated name to the parser; a map is a
                # mapping, so the last one written is what the name
                # means, in the place the first one claimed.
                fields[name] = member
                in_map = skip_blank(content, after)
                if in_map >= len(content):
                    error(f"json: the text ends inside the object opened at byte {at}")
                let mark = content.byte_at(in_map)
                if mark == comma:
                    in_map += 1
                elif mark == brace_close:
                    mapped = true
                else:
                    error(f"json: a comma or a close was expected at byte {in_map}")
        return Json.object(fields = fields), in_map + 1

    # A multi-value answer is received by a destructuring bind and
    # never passed straight through (docs/RETURNS.md), so each of these
    # names what it got before handing it on.
    if head == quote_byte:
        let spelled, after_text = try read_string(content, at)
        return Json.text(value = spelled), after_text
    if head == minus or is_digit(head):
        let counted, after_number = try read_number(content, at)
        return counted, after_number
    if word_at(content, at, "true"):
        return Json.boolean(value = true), at + 4
    if word_at(content, at, "false"):
        return Json.boolean(value = false), at + 5
    if word_at(content, at, "null"):
        return Json.null, at + 4
    error(f"json: a value was expected at byte {at}")

# RFC 8259 §2: whitespace is exactly these four bytes — space,
# horizontal tab, line feed and carriage return.  Nothing else is
# whitespace, which is why a vertical tab or a form feed between two
# tokens is an error rather than a shrug.
private func skip_blank(content: string, from: long) -> long:
    var at = from
    while at < len(content):
        let c = content.byte_at(at)
        if c != 32 and c != 9 and c != 10 and c != 13:
            return at
        at += 1
    return at

# --- strings, both directions ----------------------------------------

# RFC 8259 §7.  Answers the text the token spells and the byte after
# the closing quote.
#
# Almost every string in almost every document holds no escape at all,
# and that one is a slice of the input and needs no builder.  The first
# backslash hands the rest of the token to `read_escaped`, which does.
# The two loops repeat two of §7's sentences — a control character must
# be escaped, and a string must be closed — because they are asking
# different questions of the same bytes; folding them together would
# mean building a string for every token that did not need one.
private func read_string(content: string, from: long) -> (string, long)!:
    var at = from + 1
    while at < len(content):
        let c = content.byte_at(at)
        if c == quote_byte:
            return content[from + 1:at], at + 1
        elif c == backslash:
            let decoded, after = try read_escaped(content, from, at)
            return decoded, after
        elif c < 32:
            # §7: the control characters must be escaped.  A raw
            # newline inside a string is the usual way to meet this.
            error(f"json: an unescaped control character inside a string, at byte {at}")
        at += 1
    error(f"json: the string opened at byte {from} is never closed")

# The rest of a string token, from its first backslash, decoded.
private func read_escaped(content: string, from: long, first: long) -> (string, long)!:
    var out = new builder()
    out.append(content[from + 1:first])
    var at = first
    while at < len(content):
        let c = content.byte_at(at)
        if c == quote_byte:
            return out.build(), at + 1
        elif c == backslash:
            at = try put_decoded(out, content, at)
        elif c < 32:
            error(f"json: an unescaped control character inside a string, at byte {at}")
        else:
            # A run of ordinary bytes goes in whole: it can hold whole
            # characters of any width, and it ends at a backslash or
            # at the closing quote, both of which are ASCII.
            let run = at
            while at < len(content) and content.byte_at(at) != backslash and content.byte_at(at) != quote_byte and content.byte_at(at) >= 32:
                at += 1
            out.append(content[run:at])
    error(f"json: the string opened at byte {from} is never closed")

# RFC 8259 §7's eight escapes and `\uXXXX`.  Puts what the escape
# spells into `out` and answers the byte after it.
private func put_decoded(out: builder, content: string, from: long) -> long!:
    if from + 1 >= len(content):
        error(f"json: the text ends inside an escape, at byte {from}")
    let c = content.byte_at(from + 1)
    if c == letter_u:
        # A high surrogate is half of a codepoint and must be followed
        # by its low half (§7); a low one on its own is not a
        # codepoint at all (§8.2).  Refusing both is what keeps every
        # string this module accepts decodable into UTF-8, which is
        # the only thing a Luce string can be.
        var code = try read_hex4(content, from + 2)
        var next = from + 6
        if code >= 55296 and code <= 56319:
            if from + 7 >= len(content) or content.byte_at(from + 6) != backslash or content.byte_at(from + 7) != letter_u:
                error(f"json: a high surrogate escape at byte {from} is not followed by its low half")
            let low = try read_hex4(content, from + 8)
            if low < 56320 or low > 57343:
                error(f"json: a high surrogate escape at byte {from} is followed by something that is not a low surrogate")
            # §7's own example is this exact pair: 𝄞 is one
            # codepoint written as two escapes.
            code = 65536 + (code - 55296) * 1024 + (low - 56320)
            next = from + 12
        elif code >= 56320 and code <= 57343:
            error(f"json: a low surrogate escape stands alone at byte {from}")
        out.append(chr(code))
        return next
    elif c == quote_byte or c == backslash or c == 47:
        out.append_ascii(c)
        return from + 2
    elif c == 98:
        out.append_ascii(8)
        return from + 2
    elif c == 102:
        out.append_ascii(12)
        return from + 2
    elif c == 110:
        out.append_ascii(10)
        return from + 2
    elif c == 114:
        out.append_ascii(13)
        return from + 2
    elif c == 116:
        out.append_ascii(9)
        return from + 2
    error(f"json: unknown escape at byte {from}")

# Four hexadecimal digits, as the number they spell.
private func read_hex4(content: string, from: long) -> long!:
    if from + 4 > len(content):
        error(f"json: an escape at byte {from} wants four hexadecimal digits and the text ends first")
    var value: long = 0
    for i in range(0, 4):
        let c = content.byte_at(from + i)
        var part = -1
        if c >= digit_zero and c <= digit_nine:
            part = c - digit_zero
        elif c >= 97 and c <= 102:
            part = c - 97 + 10
        elif c >= 65 and c <= 70:
            part = c - 65 + 10
        else:
            error(f"json: an escape at byte {from} wants four hexadecimal digits")
        value = value * 16 + part
    return value

# Text as a JSON string, quotes and escapes and all (RFC 8259 §7).
#
# The two escapes that are required — the quote and the backslash —
# and the control characters, which the six short names cover where
# they can and `\u00xx` covers where they cannot.  Everything else
# goes in as itself: the solidus needs no escape (§7 permits one and
# does not ask for it), and a character outside ASCII is UTF-8 in the
# output exactly as it was in the input, which is what §8.1 recommends
# and what makes a `\u` escape unnecessary here.
#
# This is the door for a program that builds JSON text of its own out
# of a builder rather than a value, and it is the same escaping
# `write` puts around a string and a member name.
func quote(content: string) -> string:
    var out = new builder()
    put_quoted(out, content)
    return out.build()

private func put_quoted(out: builder, content: string):
    out.append_ascii(quote_byte)
    var run = 0
    var at = 0
    while at < len(content):
        let c = content.byte_at(at)
        if c == quote_byte or c == backslash or c < 32:
            if at > run:
                out.append(content[run:at])
            put_escape(out, c)
            run = at + 1
        at += 1
    out.append(content[run:])
    out.append_ascii(quote_byte)

private func put_escape(out: builder, c: byte):
    out.append_ascii(backslash)
    if c == quote_byte or c == backslash:
        out.append_ascii(c)
    elif c == 8:
        out.append_ascii(98)
    elif c == 12:
        out.append_ascii(102)
    elif c == 10:
        out.append_ascii(110)
    elif c == 13:
        out.append_ascii(114)
    elif c == 9:
        out.append_ascii(116)
    else:
        # Every other control character, four hexadecimal digits of
        # it.  Lowercase, as JavaScript and Python both write them.
        out.append_ascii(letter_u)
        out.append_ascii(digit_zero)
        out.append_ascii(digit_zero)
        out.append_ascii(hex_digit(c >> 4 & 15))
        out.append_ascii(hex_digit(c & 15))

private func hex_digit(value: int) -> int:
    if value < 10:
        return digit_zero + value
    return 97 + value - 10

# --- numbers ---------------------------------------------------------

# RFC 8259 §6, and the whole of it: an optional minus, an integer part
# that is either a single zero or a digit string not starting with
# one, an optional fraction with at least one digit, and an optional
# exponent with at least one digit.  `.5`, `5.`, `01`, `+1`, `1e`,
# `0x10`, `NaN` and `Infinity` are all outside it, and none of them
# reaches a caller as a number.
#
# The notation decides the member: written whole, it is an `integer`;
# written with a fraction or an exponent, it is a `real`.  A whole
# number too large for a `long` is a `real` too, because that is where
# its precision honestly is — and a number too large for a `double` is
# refused, since there is nothing left to hold it with.
private func read_number(content: string, from: long) -> (Json, long)!:
    var at = from
    if content.byte_at(at) == minus:
        at += 1
    if at >= len(content) or not is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
        error(f"json: a number needs a digit, at byte {at}")
    if content.byte_at(at) == digit_zero:
        at += 1
        if at < len(content) and is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
            error(f"json: a number may not have a leading zero, at byte {from}")
    else:
        while at < len(content) and is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
            at += 1
    var whole = true
    if at < len(content) and content.byte_at(at) == point:
        whole = false
        at += 1
        if at >= len(content) or not is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
            error(f"json: a fraction needs a digit after the point, at byte {at}")
        while at < len(content) and is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
            at += 1
    if at < len(content) and (content.byte_at(at) == letter_e or content.byte_at(at) == letter_e_capital):
        whole = false
        at += 1
        if at < len(content) and (content.byte_at(at) == plus or content.byte_at(at) == minus):
            at += 1
        if at >= len(content) or not is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
            error(f"json: an exponent needs a digit, at byte {at}")
        while at < len(content) and is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
            at += 1
    let token = content[from:at]
    if whole:
        let counted = parse_int(token)
        if counted != none:
            return Json.integer(value = counted), at
    let measured = parse_float(token)
    if measured != none:
        return Json.real(value = measured), at
    error(f"json: the number at byte {from} is past what a double can hold")

private func is_digit(c: byte) -> bool:
    return c >= digit_zero and c <= digit_nine

# True when `word` stands at `at`.  Byte by byte rather than by slice,
# because a slice that ended inside a character would trap and the
# text after a literal name is whatever the document says it is.
private func word_at(content: string, at: long, word: string) -> bool:
    if at + len(word) > len(content):
        return false
    for i in range(0, len(word)):
        if content.byte_at(at + i) != word.byte_at(i):
            return false
    return true

# --- writing ---------------------------------------------------------

# One value and everything under it, as JSON text.
#
# Recursive, for the reason the reader is: the tree is the shape of
# the walk.  Every member of the union is written here and there is no
# `else`, so the day a member is added this function stops compiling
# and names it.
private func put_value(out: builder, held: Json, spaces: long, depth: long):
    match held:
        null:
            out.append("null")
        boolean(value):
            out.append(string(value))
        integer(value):
            out.append(string(value))
        real(value):
            put_real(out, value)
        text(value):
            put_quoted(out, value)
        array(items):
            if len(items) == 0:
                out.append("[]")
            else:
                out.append("[")
                var first = true
                for item in items:
                    if not first:
                        out.append(",")
                    put_break(out, spaces, depth + 1)
                    put_value(out, item, spaces, depth + 1)
                    first = false
                put_break(out, spaces, depth)
                out.append("]")
        object(fields):
            if len(fields) == 0:
                out.append("{}")
            else:
                out.append("{")
                var first = true
                for name, entry in fields:
                    if not first:
                        out.append(",")
                    put_break(out, spaces, depth + 1)
                    put_quoted(out, name)
                    out.append(":")
                    if spaces > 0:
                        out.append(" ")
                    put_value(out, entry, spaces, depth + 1)
                    first = false
                put_break(out, spaces, depth)
                out.append("}")

# A real, written so that reading it back gives a real.
#
# `string(d)` answers the shortest decimal that reads back as the same
# double, which is what a JSON writer wants — but a whole-valued one
# comes out with no fraction, and `42` read back is an `integer`.  So
# the point is put on, the way Python's `json` does, and the member
# survives the round trip.
#
# What `string(d)` does *not* do is write an exponent, so `1e300`
# leaves here as its three hundred and one digits.  That is longer than
# a JSON writer would choose and it is still the same number: the text
# reads back as the identical double, which is the property this
# module owes.  Shortening it is a job for `string(double)`, where
# every other caller would get it too, and not for a private helper
# here.
private func put_real(out: builder, value: double):
    let written = string(value)
    if written.find_byte(letter_i, 0) >= 0 or written.find_byte(letter_n, 0) >= 0:
        # "inf", "-inf", "nan": RFC 8259 §6 has no grammar for any of
        # them, so there is no text to write.  Nothing this module
        # parses can get here; a value built by hand out of an
        # overflowing sum can.
        trap(f"json: {written} is not a number JSON can write")
    out.append(written)
    if written.find_byte(point, 0) < 0:
        out.append(".0")

# A line break and one level of indentation, or nothing at all when
# the caller asked for no spaces.
private func put_break(out: builder, spaces: long, depth: long):
    if spaces <= 0:
        return
    out.append_ascii(10)
    for i in range(0, spaces * depth):
        out.append_ascii(32)
